 \section{Detailed Case Analysis}
 \subsection{Ethylene Production Yield}
 The Yield source contains a percentage calculation and a non-positive-input guard:
-\par
-\begin{minipage}{\columnwidth}
-\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python,breaklines=true,basicstyle=\ttfamily\scriptsize,columns=fullflexible,keepspaces=true,showstringspaces=false,caption={Verbatim Yield source excerpt from the frozen dossier source; SHA-256 identity is retained in the supplement.}]
+\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python,breaklines=true,breakatwhitespace=true,caption={Verbatim Yield source excerpt from the frozen dossier source; SHA-256 identity is retained in the supplement.}]
 def calculate_ethylene_yield_pct(ethylene_produced_tons: float, feedstock_input_tons: float) -> float:
     """Calculates ethylene production yield as a percentage of feedstock input."""
     if feedstock_input_tons <= 0:
         return 0.0
     return (ethylene_produced_tons / feedstock_input_tons) * 100.0
 \end{lstlisting}
-\end{minipage}
-\par
 The dossier displays the positive-input ratio as its Formal Formula. The frozen source uses \texttt{feedstock\_input\_tons <= 0} and returns 0.0 on that branch. The reader-facing Logic Safeguards block omits this non-positive-input behavior. For the docstring shown in Listing~1, the generated lineage prose is ``Output alias: maps this expression to a in the result set.'' Direct source comparison identifies the statement as a function docstring, establishing the lineage-typing defect. The condition finding illustrates the criterion boundary exposed by independent review: two reviewers counted the visible source branch as disclosure, whereas one reviewer and the checker required the reader-facing safeguard layer to state the branch.
 
